** Pass 9 — Beta readers and final draft

Between two and five readers. More is not better: noise grows with numbers.

*** Who

The right reader reads what you write, can articulate *why* something does not
work —not just that they did not like it— and separates their preferences from
the text's real problems. Avoid anyone who tells you what you want to hear,
anyone who filters what they read through their own insecurities, and anyone who
leaves you hanging in uncertainty.

| Reader | Contact | Sent | Received | Fits what I write |
|--------+---------+------+----------+-------------------|
|        |         |      |          |                   |

*** What to ask

- *Structure*: holes, inconsistencies, confusion? On what page did it start to
  slow down? Is the ending satisfying? Did the transformation feel earned? Is
  any promise from the first quarter left unkept?
- *Characters*: did you care about the protagonist? Did you believe their
  decisions? Did you understand the opponent? Did any of them feel flat or
  redundant?
- *Prose*: info dumps, and where? Slow or overwritten stretches? Unnatural
  dialogue?

*** How to read it

Feedback is information, not instructions.

- *Pattern versus noise.* If three readers flag the same thing in the same
  scene, there is a real problem: fix it even if you dislike the solution they
  propose. If only one flags it, look at the context.
- *Symptom versus cause.* Readers diagnose symptoms. "Chapter 7 bored me" is a
  symptom; the cause may be a scene with no Gap, a stalled arc, or uniform pace.
  Your job is to trace the cause.
- *When it contradicts your intention.* Either your intention is not reaching
  the page, or that reader is not your reader. To tell them apart: are they
  describing what you wanted them to feel? If they do not describe it, it is an
  execution problem. If they describe it but dislike it, it is a question of
  fit.
- *What nobody flags and you know is wrong.* That feedback exists and is the
  most valuable of all. After reading the notes, sit alone with the manuscript
  and listen to your own critical voice.

